# Directory Structure — Next.js (App Router)

## Standard structure

```
app/
├── layout.tsx                  ← Root layout
├── page.tsx                    ← Home page (/)
├── globals.css
└── (routes)/
    └── <segment>/
        ├── page.tsx            ← UI for this route (Server Component)
        ├── layout.tsx          ← Nested layout (if needed)
        ├── loading.tsx         ← Suspense fallback
        ├── error.tsx           ← Error boundary
        └── actions.ts          ← Server Actions for this route

components/
├── atoms/                      ← Smallest units, no internal state (Button, Input, Badge)
├── molecules/                  ← Composed from atoms (FormField, SearchBar)
└── organisms/                  ← Complex, may have state (Header, DataTable, Form)

lib/
├── utils.ts                    ← Pure utility functions
├── constants.ts                ← App-wide constants
└── <domain>/
    └── <domain>.ts             ← Domain-specific logic

types/
└── index.ts                    ← Shared TypeScript types / interfaces
```

## Principles

- **Server Components are the default** — only add `'use client'` when needed: event handlers, browser APIs, useState/useEffect
- **Server Actions** live in `actions.ts` co-located with the route segment
- **Components** follow Atomic Design — atoms must not import molecules/organisms
- **`lib/`** contains pure logic only — no JSX
